# 1. Drift Handling & Risk Logic: The "Auto-Revert + Flag" Standard
Drift Detector — a scheduled (or manually triggered, for the PoC) re-scan comparing live RBAC state per SA against its last approved baseline in the Baseline Store. A mismatch means a permission Guardian previously revoked has reappeared, through some channel Guardian didn't perform itself. Guardian does not try to guess whether that was an attack, an admin mistake, or a legitimate emergency override — that's undecidable from cluster state alone.

If an admin (or an attacker) manually adds a dangerous permission to a cluster, you never leave it active while waiting for a human to check a dashboard. If you wait, the cluster is compromised in the interim.

The Enterprise Flow: Guardian detects the drift and instantly auto-reverts it to the approved least-privilege baseline. It immediately sends an alert to the Human Approval Queue: "Drift detected and neutralized. If this was an intentional admin change, click 'Promote to Baseline'."
If the admin clicks "Promote", Guardian updates its internal database and re-applies the new permissions safely.

give admins an explicit "pause enforcement on this SA for N minutes" control they invoke before making a change they know Guardian would otherwise revert. That way the system defaults to secure always, but doesn't fight people who are following the correct process for an emergency.
Break-Glass Lever (Emergency Override): To prevent Guardian from fighting admins during incident response, the system includes a "pause enforcement" control. Admins can pause drift-remediation on a specific ServiceAccount for N minutes. Once the N minutes expire, standard drift detection resumes; if the emergency permissions were not manually cleaned up, Guardian will auto-revert them and flag the queue.

Confidence Scores: If a change is classified as "Low Risk" but the LLM returns a "Low Confidence" score (e.g., under 80%), it must bypass the auto-apply route and go straight to the human queue. Low confidence is a high-risk signal in itself.

# 2. Database & Baseline History: Never Delete Data
Using a status enum (ACTIVE / SUPERSEDED / BROKEN_ROLLBACK / PENDING_APPROVAL / REJECTED).
Do not erase old baselines. In enterprise security, auditability is non-negotiable. If a breach happens six months from now, forensic investigators need to see the exact history of what Guardian applied and when.
How to manage it: Use a status column in your SQLite/Postgres database. A baseline record can be ACTIVE, SUPERSEDED (an older, safe version), or BROKEN_ROLLBACK (a version Guardian applied that crashed the app).

The Rollback Flow: If Guardian applies a new baseline and the app fails the health check, Guardian does not delete the bad baseline. It changes its status to BROKEN_ROLLBACK (so it is never applied again) and changes the previous SUPERSEDED baseline back to ACTIVE. You only ever feed the currently ACTIVE baseline to the LLM.

    2.1) Searching the Database for Broken Baselines with SHA-256:
Every time the LLM generates a new set of permissions (the YAML rules), your Python backend takes that specific array of rules and runs it through a SHA-256 hash function. This converts the entire YAML structure into a unique, short string (e.g., a1b2c3d4...).
You save this hash in your database column alongside the YAML.
The Check: Before Guardian applies any newly generated policy, it calculates the hash of the new policy and runs a fast SQL query: SELECT status FROM policy_baselines WHERE hash = 'a1b2c3d4...'.
If the database returns BROKEN_ROLLBACK, the system instantly knows the LLM is suggesting a policy that crashed the app in the past. It rejects the LLM's suggestion immediately and forces the LLM to try a different configuration.

    optimization (JSON Canonicalization - RFC 8785):
The Whitespace Trap: When you hash YAML, it is extremely brittle. If the LLM (or your Python template) generates the exact same rules but adds an extra space, a newline, or changes the order of the keys, the SHA-256 hash will be completely different, and your BROKEN_ROLLBACK check will fail to catch it.
The Fix: Before hashing, do not hash the raw YAML string. Instead, take the Python dictionary representing the rules, Sort the keys alphabetically, sort the list of permission rules deterministically (e.g., by apiGroup, then resource, then verb), and convert it to a minimized JSON string using json.dumps(obj, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":")). Hash this canonicalized string to guarantee identical permission sets always produce the exact same hash.

    One refinement: 
canonicalize both the sort order of keys within each permission object and the sort order of the list of permissions itself — a set of rules [A, B] and [B, A] mean the same thing semantically, so both need to normalize to the same hash. And make sure the hash column is actually indexed (CREATE INDEX idx_hash ON policy_baselines(hash)) — otherwise "fast lookup" isn't actually fast once the table grows.


# 3. The 4 Data Layers & The Audit Log Problem
How it actually works:
The LLM never reads raw audit logs. Your Python collector/ script acts as a data aggregator.

AUDIT LOGS & CIS BENCHMARK ALIGNMENT:
K8s audit policies have levels (None/Metadata/Request/RequestResponse). If you log at RequestResponse level for secrets, you'd be writing the actual secret contents into your own audit log — a real antipattern. Log at Metadata level for sensitive resources (verb/user/resource/response-code only, no body) — this is precisely what CIS's own Kubernetes benchmark recommends for exactly this reason.

Python parses the logs: The script scans the 200,000 lines and counts the occurrences.
JSON Compression: It turns 200,000 lines into a tiny JSON summary: {"service_account": "backup-sa", "used_verbs": ["get", "list"], "unused_verbs": ["create", "delete"], "timeframe": "72_hours"}.
LLM Ingestion: You only feed that tiny aggregated JSON to the LLM.

How the 4 layers synergize to protect Disaster Recovery (DR):
Audit Logs tell the LLM: "This identity has used 0 permissions in 30 days." (Looks useless).
Dynamic API tells the LLM: "This identity currently has secrets: get." (Looks dangerous).
Static Manifests tell the LLM: "This role was named disaster-recovery-controller by the developers in Git."
The LLM's Reasoning: The LLM combines these. It knows what "disaster recovery" means. It concludes: "Usage is 0, but the manifest indicates emergency infrastructure. Revoking this could break incident response. Verdict: keep_with_alert."
SubjectAccessReviews (SAR): Guardian uses SAR at the very end to definitively test that the blast radius is contained before and after any changes.


AUDIT LOGS:
A single event looks something like this:
{"stage": "ResponseComplete", "verb": "get", "user": {"username": "system:serviceaccount:default:backup-sa"}, "objectRef": {"resource": "secrets"}}

    the work of collector/ script:
Step 1: Parse and Filter: The Python script reads the raw /var/log/audit.log file. It ignores failed requests and only looks for lines where stage is ResponseComplete and the HTTP response code is >= 200 and < 300 (2xx codes). Filtering strictly for 200 drops successful POST (201) and DELETE (204) operations.
Step 2: Group by Identity: The script grabs the user.username field to identify the ServiceAccount.
Step 3: Count and Compress: Instead of keeping 5,000 separate log lines saying the backup-sa read a secret, Python uses a simple dictionary to tally it up: {"secrets": {"get": 5000}}.
Step 4: Cross-Reference: The script pulls the live RBAC permissions (what the ServiceAccount is allowed to do). It subtracts what was actually used (from Step 3) from what is allowed.
Step 5: The Final JSON: Python spits out a tiny, heavily compressed JSON string:
JSON
{
  "service_account": "backup-sa",
  "time_window_analyzed": "72_hours",
  "used_permissions": [{"resource": "secrets", "verb": "get"}],
  "unused_permissions": [{"resource": "pods", "verb": "delete"}]
}
This tiny block is the only thing the LLM reads.


# 4. Where the Human Review Happens (The Streamlit Dashboard)
The Database State: When the orchestrator encounters a high-risk change or detects drift, it inserts the proposed YAML into the database with a status of PENDING_APPROVAL.
The Dashboard View: create a page in Streamlit called "Approval Queue". This page constantly polls the database for any records marked PENDING_APPROVAL.
The UI: The Streamlit page displays a clean, side-by-side diff.
Left Side: The current active baseline.
Right Side: The newly proposed changes (or the unauthorized drift).
The Buttons: Below the diff, place two Streamlit buttons: [Approve / Promote to Baseline] and [Reject / Revert].
The Action: When the admin clicks Approve, Streamlit sends a quick HTTP POST request to your Python backend (in the backend/ folder). The backend updates the database status to ACTIVE and applies the YAML to the kind cluster.


